{% block content %}
<header class="hero page-hero" id="top">
  <h1>TDIUC</h1>
  <p class="role">Task Directed Image Understanding Challenge</p>
  <p class="email">A more nuanced analysis of visual question answering algorithms &middot; ICCV 2017</p>
</header>

<section id="about" class="section">
  <div class="wrap narrow">
    <figure class="figure"><img src="../images/vqa-tasks.png" alt="Examples of the 12 TDIUC question types"></figure>
    <p>
      Visual question answering (VQA) is a challenging vision and language problem that demands a wide range of
      language and image understanding abilities. However, studying the abilities of current VQA systems is very
      difficult due to critical problems with bias and a lack of well-annotated question types. The Task Directed
      Image Understanding Challenge (TDIUC) divides VQA into 12 constituent tasks, making it easier to measure and
      compare the performance of VQA algorithms.
    </p>
    <p>
      VQA is interesting because it encompasses many other computer vision problems, e.g., object detection, object
      classification, attribute classification, positional reasoning, and counting. Truly solving VQA requires an
      algorithm to solve all of them. Because prior datasets are heavily unbalanced toward certain kinds of
      questions, good performance on less frequent kinds of questions has negligible impact on overall performance.
      For example, object presence questions are far more common than questions requiring positional reasoning, so an
      algorithm that excels at positional reasoning cannot showcase its abilities. TDIUC's metrics compensate for
      this bias, so good performance on TDIUC requires good performance across kinds of questions. Many questions in
      other datasets can also be answered from the question alone, so TDIUC introduces <em>absurd</em> questions that
      require an algorithm to look at the image to decide whether the question even applies.
    </p>
    <h3>TDIUC at a glance</h3>
    <ul class="stats">
      <li><strong>12</strong> question types <span>grouped by underlying task, including absurd questions</span></li>
      <li><strong>167,437</strong> images <span>from MS-COCO and Visual Genome</span></li>
      <li><strong>1,654,167</strong> question-answer pairs <span>from three distinct sources</span></li>
      <li><strong>4</strong> new evaluation metrics <span>designed to compensate for bias</span></li>
      <li><strong>6</strong> new experimental setups <span>to answer crucial questions about VQA algorithms</span></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="paper" class="section alt">
  <div class="wrap narrow">
    <h2>Paper</h2>
    <figure class="figure"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.09684"><img src="../images/kushalkafle_iccv2017.png" alt="TDIUC paper preview"></a></figure>
    <p>
      In our ICCV paper, we show how TDIUC enables a more nuanced analysis and comparison of VQA algorithms through
      extensive experimentation. If you use TDIUC in your work, please cite:
    </p>
<pre class="cite">@inproceedings{kafle2017analysis,
  title={An Analysis of Visual Question Answering Algorithms},
  author={Kafle, Kushal and Kanan, Christopher},
  booktitle={ICCV},
  year={2017}
}</pre>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="download" class="section">
  <div class="wrap narrow">
    <h2>Download and Usage</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="../data/TDIUC.zip">Download TDIUC</a> (70 MB). The archive contains all question-answer pairs for the
      train and validation splits, manually created images, scripts for downloading and setting up images from MS-COCO
      and Visual Genome, and a script for computing the normalized and un-normalized MPT scores from the paper. See the
      included README for setup instructions.
    </p>
    <p>
      The format mirrors the <a href="https://visualqa.org/vqa_v1_download.html">VQA dataset</a>, so code written for
      VQA works with TDIUC with little or no modification.
    </p>
    <p class="warning">
      <strong>Note:</strong> the validation/test split of TDIUC uses question-answer pairs and images from the VQA
      validation set and images from the Visual Genome validation set. Do not use these sources as additional training
      data if you evaluate on TDIUC.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

